WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Daily Weather Swings Raise Glacier C-Level Contribution 5-10% by 2100. This is Planetarian Daily, Episode 218. ETH Zurich and Vrya University, Brussels, pushed the global glacier evolution model to daily resolution. The update covered more than 12,500 glaciers that hold 94% of the world's glacier ice. In a Nature Communications study covered by Fizz.org. The daily run reproduced observed mass loss during recent extreme

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.980
melt years in the Alps. North America, Svalbard, and Central Asia better than monthly versions.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.980
Lead author Landervan Tricked noted that glaciers respond to individual days rather than monthly

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:48.160
averages. Co-author Harry Zekolari added that resolving daily variability improves understanding

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:53.280
of meltwater runoff timing that affects downstream water resources for millions of people.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:59.800
Those single-day hits stack across a season. They also stack across regions, not only on one ice

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.940
field. Downstream, the volume of freshwater coming off the ice shifts with those same

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:10.160
short bursts of weather. Water resource planning in glacier-fed basins may need to account

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:13.140
for more variable seasonal flows than previously modeled.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:18.080
One open question is whether similar daily forcing adjustments would change projections

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:24.128
for other cryosphere components, such as permafrost. Something most people get wrong is that

00:01:24.128 --> 00:01:29.368
glaciers only answer to slow drifts in yearly average temperature. A handful of unusually

00:01:29.368 --> 00:01:35.228
warm days or a single extreme burst of precipitation, can strip protective snow and switch the surface

00:01:35.228 --> 00:01:40.888
into a much faster melting state. High albedo snow reflects incoming sunlight while it lasts.

00:01:41.628 --> 00:01:47.328
Lose that snow and darker ice takes in far more solar energy, melt then feeds on itself in a

00:01:47.328 --> 00:01:53.028
self-reinforcing loop. Monthly averages smear those peak days into a milder mean and miss the

00:01:53.028 --> 00:01:58.488
moment the cover fails. The daily version matched actual mass loss measurements during documented

00:01:58.488 --> 00:02:05.368
extreme years. It matched 2022 in the European Alps, it matched 2023 in Western North America,

00:02:05.948 --> 00:02:13.168
it matched 2024 in Svalbard, it matched 2025 in Central Asia, and it matched 2026 in the Alps

00:02:13.168 --> 00:02:19.268
again. Rain near freezing speeds run off instead of adding mass. Snow near that same threshold can

00:02:19.268 --> 00:02:24.128
still protect the surface. That choice changes how much mass a glacier keeps or sheds.

00:02:24.128 --> 00:02:29.508
For anyone living downstream of glacier-fed rivers, the timing and reliability of summer

00:02:29.508 --> 00:02:33.028
water supplies could shift more than earlier monthly models suggested.

00:02:33.768 --> 00:02:38.128
Putting those short-term processes in the model would give water managers clearer guidance

00:02:38.128 --> 00:02:40.288
on when peak flows are likely to occur.

00:02:41.329 --> 00:02:46.329
Bats carry enhanced viral immunity pathways that appear linked to unusually long lifespans

00:02:46.329 --> 00:02:49.469
relative to body size, according to the scientist.

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:54.009
The connection suggests immune adaptations shape longevity traits.

00:02:54.469 --> 00:02:57.889
Bats maintain low levels of inflammation while they control viruses.

00:02:58.589 --> 00:03:01.869
The same viruses would trigger damaging responses in other mammals.

00:03:02.489 --> 00:03:08.369
The work ties that combination of viral control and restrained inflammation to how long bats live for their size.

00:03:09.664 --> 00:03:14.584
Serotonergic psychedelics in a separate immune meta-analysis picked up on the science subreddit.

00:03:14.984 --> 00:03:17.904
Modulate responses mainly in inflammatory settings.

00:03:17.904 --> 00:03:21.244
Effects stayed small under healthy baseline conditions.

00:03:21.564 --> 00:03:26.164
The pattern points to a context-dependent mechanism rather than broad immunosuppression.

00:03:26.764 --> 00:03:31.224
Early data suggests the compounds may act differently when inflammation is already present.

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:34.484
They do not look the same against a normal immune state.

00:03:35.554 --> 00:03:39.594
Sailing injections that separate the median nerve from surrounding tissue were tested

00:03:39.594 --> 00:03:40.494
for carpal tunnel.

00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:46.054
A clinical trial covered on the science subreddit found that approach outperformed steroid injections.

00:03:46.054 --> 00:03:46.774
symptoms.

00:03:46.774 --> 00:03:49.654
Symptom relief lasted up to six months after the procedure.

00:03:50.154 --> 00:03:52.334
The method physically frees the trapped nerve.

00:03:52.834 --> 00:03:55.574
It does not rely on anti-inflammatory effects alone.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:01.530
Exclusive breastfeeding for six months correlated with reduced attention deficit hyperactivity

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:06.370
disorder, or ADHD, symptoms even after adjusting for genetic risk.

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:11.290
Coverage on the science subreddit noted that the protective association persisted across

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:12.370
the cohort examined.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:17.290
The findings suggest early nutrition may influence neurodevelopmental trajectories.

00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:20.690
That influence showed up independently of inherited factors.

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:25.930
The adjustment for genetic risk is what separates this correlation from a simple family history

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:26.510
story.

00:04:27.371 --> 00:04:32.951
A denovo DNM1L mutation blocked normal mitochondrial division in a child with epileptic

00:04:32.951 --> 00:04:35.551
and cephalopathy and refractory seizures.

00:04:36.231 --> 00:04:40.791
Researchers reporting on the science subreddit linked that mutation to impaired mitochondrial

00:04:40.791 --> 00:04:41.151
fission.

00:04:41.151 --> 00:04:45.111
Fission is required for proper organelle function inside cells.

00:04:45.511 --> 00:04:48.391
It is also required for distribution of those organelles.

00:04:48.891 --> 00:04:52.811
The case ties a failure of that division step to severe neurological disease.

00:04:53.797 --> 00:04:59.077
A new spatial genomics method maps long-range DNA interactions, while keeping each cells

00:04:59.077 --> 00:05:01.457
original location inside intact tissue.

00:05:01.937 --> 00:05:07.257
The technique described in a Fizz.org report addresses how three-dimensional chromatin

00:05:07.257 --> 00:05:09.497
folding is regulated in native settings.

00:05:09.497 --> 00:05:14.297
It lets researchers study nuclear organization without losing spatial relationships.

00:05:14.757 --> 00:05:17.697
Those relationships are the ones that exist inside organs.

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:19.877
They also exist inside tumors.

00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:25.827
Higher atmospheric carbon dioxide altered protein profiles across wheat cultivars in a

00:05:25.827 --> 00:05:27.227
libnitz institute study.

00:05:27.807 --> 00:05:33.367
Baking quality shifted and so did potential tolerability in the Fizz.org account of that work.

00:05:34.027 --> 00:05:36.207
Different cultivars responded in different ways.

00:05:36.207 --> 00:05:41.327
The study concludes that adaptation efforts must track quality traits alongside yield.

00:05:42.047 --> 00:05:46.007
Breathing choices will need to balance nutritional and functional properties under future

00:05:46.007 --> 00:05:46.907
climate conditions.

00:05:47.943 --> 00:05:52.403
Brief contact with healthy clones restored fully bleached corals to health within seven

00:05:52.403 --> 00:05:54.023
weeks in laboratory conditions.

00:05:54.503 --> 00:05:58.823
A Fizz.org report calls that recovery rate the fastest documented to date.

00:05:59.363 --> 00:06:01.343
The method uses live tissue transfer.

00:06:01.783 --> 00:06:04.703
That transfer restarted symbiotic algae populations.

00:06:04.703 --> 00:06:07.683
The restart happened before the coral host died.

00:06:08.779 --> 00:06:13.219
Footprints at White Sands National Park record human presence during the coldest phase of

00:06:13.219 --> 00:06:16.499
the last Ice Age, roughly 23,000 years ago.

00:06:17.099 --> 00:06:21.019
Fizz.org coverage shows people navigated abrupt environmental changes.

00:06:21.539 --> 00:06:24.639
The tracks preserve evidence of repeated visits to the lake shore.

00:06:25.279 --> 00:06:27.779
Those visits continued as conditions fluctuated.

00:06:28.219 --> 00:06:32.119
The record sits in the coldest stretch of that Ice Age, not a milder interval.

00:06:32.119 --> 00:06:38.042
Before we go, continued collection of daily mass-balanced data from additional glacier regions

00:06:38.042 --> 00:06:42.482
would help test whether the same short-term processes dominate under different climate

00:06:42.482 --> 00:06:42.882
regimes.

